 "drivingQuestion": "How can one hold an acquired privilege for a damaging dovecote?",
 "outcome": "resolved",
 "outcomeNote": "Rav Nahman's rule limited to severe damages",
 "continuesQuestionOf": null,
 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "bava_batra.23a.11",
   "daf": "23a",
   "sentenceId": 10,
   "he": "הֲרֵי הוּא בְּחֶזְקָתוֹ. וְהָא אָמַר רַב נַחְמָן אָמַר רַבָּה בַּר אֲבוּהּ: אֵין חֲזָקָה לִנְזָקִין! רַב מָרִי אָמַר: בְּקוּטְרָא, רַב זְבִיד אָמַר: בְּבֵית הַכִּסֵּא.",
   "en": "§ The mishna teaches that if one bought the dovecote with the land, «he has the acquired privilege» of use. The Gemara asks: «But doesn’t Rav Naḥman say» that «Rabba bar Avuh says: There is no acquired privilege» of use «for» cases of «damage?» Why should he retain his acquired privilege of use when his doves cause damage? «Rav Mari said:» Rav Naḥman’s statement is referring specifically «to smoke,» which causes serious damage, and that is why it overrides an acquired privilege. «Rav Zevid said:» It is referring «to a bathroom,» whose odor is particularly strong."
  }
 ],
